.TH AENIGMA-LOCK-KEY 8 "2026-08-17" "aenigma 5.0.0" "Aenigma Administration Scripts"
.SH NAME
aenigma-lock-key \- encrypt the aenigma service's private key with a passphrase
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aenigma-lock-key
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aenigma-lock-key
re-encrypts the existing
.I aenigma
service private key in place, wrapping it with AES-256 encryption via
.BR openssl (1).
It is intended to be run against a private key that is currently stored
unencrypted (or that should be re-locked with a new passphrase), turning
it into a passphrase-protected key file.
.PP
The key is written first to a temporary
.I locked
file, and only moved over the original private key file once
.BR openssl (1)
has succeeded, so a failed or interrupted encryption does not leave the
original private key truncated or corrupted. Ownership and permissions on
the resulting file are then reset to the
.I aenigma
service user, mode 0600.
.PP
The script takes no options or arguments; it always operates on the
service's single, fixed private key path.
.B aenigma-lock-key
must be run as root, since it changes ownership of the private key file to
the
.I aenigma
service account.
.PP
.BR openssl (1)
will interactively prompt for the new encryption passphrase (and its
confirmation) during the operation. This script is not suitable for
unattended/non-interactive use as written.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/aenigma/private-key.pem
The service's RSA private key. Must already exist; this is both the input
key that gets encrypted and, on success, the final output location.
.TP
.I /var/lib/aenigma/private-key-locked.pem
Temporary file holding the newly encrypted key while
.BR openssl (1)
runs. Moved over
.I private-key.pem
on success; does not persist afterward.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; the private key was re-encrypted and its ownership/permissions
were set.
.TP
.B 1
The script was not invoked as root, or
.I private-key.pem
does not exist.
.PP
Any failure from
.BR openssl (1)
causes the script to abort immediately, since it runs with
.B set -e
and does not trap or ignore command failures. Because the original key is
only overwritten after
.BR openssl (1)
succeeds, an aborted run leaves the original private key file untouched.
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
.B aenigma-lock-key
does not verify whether
.I private-key.pem
is already passphrase-protected before running. If it is, OpenSSL will
first prompt for the existing passphrase to decrypt it, then prompt again
for the new one to re-encrypt it \(em effectively allowing this script to
also be used to change an existing passphrase.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no corresponding "unlock" step provided by this script; removing
encryption from the key requires a separate, explicit
.BR openssl (1)
invocation outside of this tool.
.IP \(bu 2
.B umask 077
is set for the duration of the script, so the temporary locked-key file is
created with owner-only access even before the explicit
.BR chmod (1)
call runs.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR aenigma-keys (8),
.BR openssl (1),
.BR umask (2)
